Recall that $a^m\div a^n=a^{m-n}$ for positive integers $m>n$ and $a^m\cdot a^n=a^{m+n}$. Now we can write $5^5\div5^4-5^3+5^2\cdot5^1$ as $5^1-5^3+5^3$. Using the definition of subtraction and the associative property of addition, we get \begin{align*}
5^1-5^3+5^3&=5^1+-5^3+5^3\\
&=5^1+(-5^3+5^3)\\
&=5^1+0\\
&=\boxed{5}.
\end{align*}

The diagram on the left shows the path of Bill's walk. As the diagram on the right illustrates, he could also have walked from $A$ to $B$ by first walking 1 mile south then $\frac{3}{4}$ mile east. [asy]
pair a=(0,1), b=(.75, 0), c=(0,.5), d=(.75,.5), o=(0,0);
draw(a--b--d--c--cycle);
label("$A$", a, NW);
